From: 3on3NHL-recomp
Subject: [PATCH] D3D12: don't copy all-zero stencil in render target transfers

On hosts without pixel shader stencil reference output (NVIDIA), transferring a
depth render target to another one with different MSAA or pitch copies the
stencil with 8 extra draws, one per bit. This game flips a shadow depth buffer
between 1x and 4x MSAA 14 times a frame (each flip being a depth clear), so at 3x
resolution these stencil copies took about 1 ms of GPU time per frame - while
the stencil there is always zero.

Track per EDRAM tile whether the stencil of the depth data there may be nonzero.
Draws that may write a nonzero stencil value, clears with a nonzero stencil and
transfers of color data to depth set it; draws that certainly write zero to the
stencil of every sample of whole tiles clear it - a single axis-aligned
rectangle (rectangle list, no clipping, culling, polygon modes, discards, alpha
test or sample mask) with the stencil test always passing, all 8 bits written
with zero, and the depth test disabled or always passing. Its covered rectangle
is found by running the vertex shader on the CPU (DrawExtentEstimator). When the
source stencil of a transfer is all zero, the stencil clear done before the
per-bit draws is the whole copy, so those draws are skipped.

diff --git a/include/rex/graphics/pipeline/render_target/cache.h b/include/rex/graphics/pipeline/render_target/cache.h
